      "property_id": "PROP-current-fina-inv-014",
      "review_verdict": "CONFIRMED_VULNERABILITY",
      "original_classification": "potential-vulnerability",
      "adjusted_severity": "High",
      "reviewer_notes": "All 3 gates passed. Gate 1: ensure_liquidate_borrow_allowed is defined at market.move:927 and called at market.move:722 (non-test caller exists). Gate 2: no trust_assumptions defined in BUG_BOUNTY_SCOPE.json; attack originates from an external, untrusted liquidator. Gate 3: contracts/protocol/sources/internal/market/market.move is explicitly in-scope and liquidation logic is a listed focus area. The module NatSpec at liquidate.move:2 states 'liquidation amount should be no bigger than the amount that would decrease the risk level of obligation to 1', but ensure_liquidate_borrow_allowed enforces only close_factor * debt_balance (e.g. 50%), which for a barely-underwater position can be orders of magnitude larger than the soft-liquidation target. An attacker can trigger this via the public liquidation entry point by calling liquidate on a barely-underwater position (weighted_risk=1.001) and repaying up to close_factor*debt (5,000 USD on a 10,000 USD debt), causing the borrower to lose ~4,970 USD more collateral than the minimum required to restore solvency, directly violating the protocol's own stated soft-liquidation invariant. Severity retained at High: direct financial loss to borrowers, triggerable by any liquidator, no external preconditions beyond a barely-underwater position.",
      "spec_reference": "SG-006 / liquidate.move:2 \u2014 'adopts soft liquidation. Liquidation amount should be no bigger than the amount that would decrease the risk level of obligation to 1.'"
    }
